==================== THE ASK ====================
Propose exactly {{ k }} ideas AS A SET. Each idea must use a DIFFERENT combination of cards. Spread the set: some combinations the account runs often, some it rarely runs, some it has never run. Label each idea's typicality relative to this account's history: common | uncommon | rare. At least one idea must be rare.

For every idea, cite what you are responding to using [card:slug], [history:ref], and [signal:ref] tokens from above. A signal on a failed attempt is raw material: an objection the audience raised is a candidate for the objection-flip card, a misreading is a candidate hook, a joke is a candidate tone.

Respect every constraint in the brief. Keep the headline under {{ headline_chars }} characters and the description under {{ description_chars }} characters.

Use only facts, figures, and customer numbers that appear in the brief or in history above. Where a claim needs a figure the brief does not give, write [number] for the team to fill in rather than inventing one. Invented statistics, test results, or shipment counts fail the policy screen.

Output {{ k }} blocks in exactly this format and nothing else:

<idea>
<cards>slug, slug[, slug...]</cards>
<typicality>common | uncommon | rare</typicality>
<reasoning>Why this combination fits this brief and world state, and what in history it responds to. Cite [card:slug] [history:ref] [signal:ref].</reasoning>
<angle>Hook: ... / Enemy: ...
Promise: ...</angle>
<copy>primary_text: ...
headline: ...
description: ...
cta: ...</copy>
<visual_brief>One paragraph describing the image.</visual_brief>
</idea>
